{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J9 — Make the DC transport actually drive a writing reconcile pass\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nStory J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1] landed the DC backend's port conformance and made it reachable\nthrough config, then drove it against a REAL Jira Data Center instance for the first time (GitHub\nActions run 30604756524). The writing path CRASHES:\n\n    ERROR: reconcile_once raised: 'JiraDataCenterTransport' object has no attribute\n    'set_entity_property'\n\n`--mode dry-run` passes; `--mode bootstrap-strict` exits 1. So epic e369's headline capability —\nJira Server/Data Center support — does not work yet, and the epic cannot honestly close without this.\n\nFiled as [rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5]; this story is its implementation.\n\n## The root cause is a PORT defect, and the gap is TWELVE members\n\nAn AST audit over `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/*.py` — collecting every method invoked on a\n`client`/`transport` receiver, diffed against `JiraDataCenterTransport`'s methods and against every\nProtocol in `_backend.py` — measured the real gap:\n\n**Called by the core, ABSENT from the DC transport (12):**\n`delete_issue`, `delete_issue_link`, `get_comments`, `get_issue_by_rest`, `get_issue_links`,\n`get_parent_map`, `remove_label`, `set_entity_property`, `set_issue_property`, `set_parent`,\n`set_reporter`, `validate_assignee_exists`.\n\n**Declared on NO Protocol in `_backend.py`: the same 12.**\n\n**The dominant failure mode is SILENT DEGRADATION, not crashing** — which inverts the obvious framing\nof this story. Classifying every call site by whether an enclosing `try` swallows `Exception`:\n\n| behaviour | count | members |\n|---|---|---|\n| propagates (crashes) | 1 | `get_issue_by_rest` (`set_reporter` was in this bucket in an earlier draft — it is not reached on DC at all today, see below) |\n| swallowed at EVERY site | 7 | `delete_issue_link`, `get_comments`, `get_issue_links`, `set_issue_property`, `set_parent`, `validate_assignee_exists`, `get_parent_map` (the last via `hasattr`, `fetcher.py:448`) |\n| MIXED — crashes at some sites, swallowed at others | 3 | `delete_issue`, `remove_label`, `set_entity_property` |\n\nSo a DC deployment does not mostly crash: it mostly **appears to converge while quietly not syncing\ncomments, links, parents, issue properties, or validating assignees.** `set_entity_property` is\nswallowed at 2 of its 3 sites and crashes at the third — which is the ONLY reason any of this was\ndiscovered. The crash was the lucky case.\n\nThis has a direct consequence for the acceptance bar: \"no `AttributeError`\" is the WRONG criterion,\nbecause it is already true at most sites and true for the wrong reason. Fixing only the crash would\nproduce a green live run with the silent gaps fully intact — a worse outcome than today, because the\ngreen run would be taken as proof the capability works.\n\n### Per-method triage is required — a blanket rule is wrong\n\nThree of the twelve need a decision that \"implement it like Cloud does\" actively gets WRONG. Each was\nverified in source, and they establish that this story is TRIAGE, not bulk translation:\n\n* **`get_parent_map` — Cloud's implementation is NOT portable.** `acli_graph.py:180` uses\n  `POST /rest/api/3/search/jql` with an opaque `nextPageToken` CURSOR, and its own docstring records\n  (ticket 8b25, live-proven) that the legacy `/rest/api/3/search` is RETIRED with HTTP 410 and that\n  **sending `startAt` is rejected with HTTP 400**. That endpoint and pagination model are Cloud-v3\n  only. DC REST v2 has `POST /rest/api/2/search` with OFFSET paging (`startAt`/`maxResults`). So the\n  Approach's \"read each Cloud counterpart first\" is actively misleading here: copying Cloud yields a\n  404/400 against DC. This one needs a v2 offset-paged implementation written fresh.\n* **`set_reporter` — RESOLVED by measurement, and the answer is three-part.** (a) The CALL SITE needs\n  no change: `jira_account_id` returns whatever `external_id` the family stored, and since bug 5f48\n  merged, DC identities are minted under provider `jira` keyed on the DC USERNAME. Measured —\n  `jira_account_id('<identity-ticket-id>') -> 'dcuser'`, while bare username / display name / email\n  all return `None`. So the seam is already vendor-neutral and hands back exactly what DC needs;\n  `set_reporter` is CONDITIONALLY reachable (on tickets whose reporter is an identity id), not\n  unreachable. (b) The TRANSPORT method is needed with a DC payload:\n  `issue.update(fields={'reporter': {'name': …}})`. (c) **The defect neither the plan nor the review\n  had:** `inbound_fields._identity_of` (:163) builds the canonical identity from `raw.get(\"accountId\")`\n  ONLY. Measured against both real shapes, a DC reporter yields `account_id=None` while display/email\n  survive — so `_diff_reporter` (`outbound_field_diff.py:105-111`) re-emits `changed[\"reporter\"]` on\n  EVERY pass, even straight after a successful write, because the next snapshot still reads `None`.\n  Implementing the transport method WITHOUT fixing `_identity_of` converts a silent no-op into visible\n  infinite churn and fails this story's own idempotence AC.\n* **`set_entity_property`** is the one with live evidence, and it is the story's spine — but note it\n  is swallowed at 2 of its 3 sites, so fixing it does NOT make the other two observable.\n\n### The exception-model gap — the core catches a Cloud-SPECIFIC error type\n\nThe DC transport raises `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` (pycontribs). Two core call sites catch only\n`urllib.error.HTTPError`, which is what Cloud's urllib-based transport raises. Verified in source:\n\n* `dispatch_apply_phases.py:119` — `except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:` around\n  `client.set_reporter(...)`. Its own comment calls a 4xx \"the common case\" (Modify-Reporter not\n  granted) and exists to DEGRADE SOFTLY. A DC `JIRAError` does not match, so it escapes and breaks the\n  whole update instead of degrading.\n* `outbound_differ.py:138-141` — `_safe_get_issue` maps `HTTPError.code == 404` to `_DELETED` and\n  `URLError/TimeoutError/OSError` to `_TRANSPORT_ERROR`. A DC `JIRAError` matches NEITHER, so a\n  remotely-deleted issue is not classified as deleted AND the exception escapes entirely. Deletion\n  detection silently breaks on DC.\n\nThis is the SAME ROOT as the missing-members defect: the port declares no exception contract, so the\ncore reaches for a transport-specific type. `urllib.error.HTTPError` in a vendor-neutral core module is\na Cloud leak.\n\n**Fix at the ADAPTER boundary** — the DC transport translates `JIRAError` into the neutral types the\ncore already handles (carrying `.code` so the 404 → `_DELETED` classification keeps working), rather\nthan teaching every core call site a second vendor's exception. Adapters adapt; the core should not\ngrow a `except JIRAError` per backend. Where a neutral type does not exist yet, declare one on the port.\n\n### The triage, DECIDED — verdict per member\n\nRecorded here so the executor implements rather than researches. Each verdict is backed by the call\nsite and by DC REST **v2** (not Cloud v3). \"IMPLEMENT\" means a normal v2 call; the two hard cases are\ncalled out explicitly.\n\n| member | verdict | basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| `set_entity_property` | IMPLEMENT | `client.add_issue_property(issue, key, data)`. VERIFIED PRESENT at RUNTIME in jira 3.10.5. Reproduce: `python -c \"import jira,inspect; print(inspect.signature(jira.JIRA.add_issue_property), inspect.getsourcefile(jira.JIRA.add_issue_property))\"` → `(self, issue: str, key: str, data) -> Response` in `jira/client.py`; body is `self._session.put(self._get_url(f\"issue/{issue}/properties/{key}\"), …)`. **A STATIC symbol lookup will NOT find it** — it is wrapped in `@translate_resource_args`, so a resolver reading the AST sees the decorator, not the `def`. Two review rounds reported it absent for this reason; the same class of false-negative as [rebar:dbf9-3fc8-625d-4730]. Verify at runtime, not statically. (It is correctly absent from OUR codebase — that is what this story adds.) Value must reach the API **verbatim** (Cloud bug 0b27: wrapping in `{\"value\": …}` silently stored the wrong shape) |\n| `set_issue_property` | IMPLEMENT | same endpoint — Cloud's `set_entity_property` is literally an alias of it (`acli_rest.py:266`) |\n| `delete_issue` | IMPLEMENT | `client.issue(key).delete()` |\n| `delete_issue_link` | IMPLEMENT | `client.delete_issue_link(id)` |\n| `get_comments` | IMPLEMENT | `client.comments(key)` — the transport ALREADY calls this inside `get_comment_map` (transport.py:327), so this is missing SURFACE, not missing capability |\n| `get_issue_by_rest` | IMPLEMENT | `client.issue(key)` — already used by `get_issue` (transport.py:234); return the RAW dict, never leak a `jira.Issue` |\n| `get_issue_links` | IMPLEMENT | read `issuelinks` off the issue fields; `JiraDataCenterBackend.map_remote_links` already canonicalizes that exact shape |\n| `remove_label` | IMPLEMENT | `issue.update(update={\"labels\": [{\"remove\": <label>}]})` via the LIBRARY — mirrors the existing `add_label`, which uses `Issue.add_field_value` |\n| `validate_assignee_exists` | IMPLEMENT — **not** `NotImplementedError` | `outbound_assignee.py:107-115` branches on `type(exc).__name__ == \"AssigneeNotFoundError\"`: that exact type ALREADY EXISTS in the DC transport (`transport.py:43`, subclassing `BackendAssigneeNotFoundError`) and is already raised by `_assign`. A `NotImplementedError` would fall to the `(None, False, False)` non-authoritative branch on EVERY DC assignee resolution — a permanent silent degradation to string-matching. Implement via `client.search_users(...)` (already used by `backend._search_users_by_username`), raising the existing `AssigneeNotFoundError` on a definitive miss |\n| `get_parent_map` | IMPLEMENT, v2 shape ONLY | `client.search_issues(jql, fields=['parent'], startAt=…, maxResults=…)` — the library's OFFSET paging is DC-native. Do **not** port Cloud's `/rest/api/3/search/jql` cursor loop; its own docstring records that `startAt` is rejected there with HTTP 400 |\n| `set_parent` | **SPIKE-GATED** (a fourth verdict category, distinct from the other three) | On DC, a SUB-TASK's parent is `fields.parent`, but EPIC membership is the \"Epic Link\" **custom field** (`customfield_NNNNN`, instance-specific), not `parent`. rebar models epics as ordinary parents, so a naive `fields.parent` write may silently no-op for epic links. SPIKE EXIT CRITERIA, all answerable in ONE CI harness run: (1) `GET /rest/api/2/field` lists a field named \"Epic Link\" → record its `customfield_NNNNN` id; (2) `client.add_issues_to_epic(<epic>, [<key>])` returns 2xx against the harness; (3) whether it takes the epic's KEY or numeric id; (4) whether a subsequent inbound fetch reports the link, i.e. it round-trips. If (1) or (2) fails, the verdict becomes `NotImplementedError` naming the epic-link limitation. There is no open-ended research: four questions, one run, a pre-decided fallback |\n| `set_reporter` | IMPLEMENT + fix `_identity_of`; NO call-site change | RESOLVED by measurement, see below. `issue.update(fields={'reporter': {'name': …}})` (DC has no accountId), AND `inbound_fields._identity_of` must read DC's `name` — without that the reporter re-emits on every pass and the idempotence AC fails |\n\n**Collateral finding, worth its own note:** `outbound_assignee.py:113` classifies by\n`type(exc).__name__ == \"AssigneeNotFoundError\"` — a STRING comparison on a class name, not an\n`isinstance` against the vendor-neutral `BackendAssigneeNotFoundError` that both adapters already\nsubclass. It works, but it silently breaks on any rename and bypasses the neutral base class that\nexists precisely for this. Flag it; fixing it is optional here and should not expand this story.\n\nSo each of the twelve needs a recorded verdict — IMPLEMENT (with the DC v2 shape, not Cloud's),\nNOT-APPLICABLE (`NotImplementedError` naming the limitation), or UNREACHABLE-BY-CALL-SITE (with the\nupstream decision named) — backed by the call-site evidence, not by symmetry with Cloud.\n\nA METHODOLOGICAL NOTE, because it changed the answer twice: an audit that matches only\n`client.method(...)` CALL forms under-counts, because the reconciler frequently passes the method as\na VALUE — `_call_with_retry(client.delete_issue_link, link_id)` (`dispatch_one.py:731`),\n`_call_with_retry(client.set_entity_property, ...)` (`dispatch_one.py:321`). The audit must collect\nevery ATTRIBUTE ACCESS on a `client`/`transport` receiver. A first pass using the narrow pattern\nreported ten and missed `delete_issue_link` and `set_parent`; the corrected pass reports twelve.\n\nAll twelve ARE implemented for Cloud, spread across `adapters/jira/acli_rest.py`, `acli.py` and\n`acli_graph.py`.\n\nSo `set_entity_property` is not \"the\" missing method — it is the FIRST one a live writing pass\nhappened to reach. Eleven more sit behind it. **Implementing only `set_entity_property` would move the\ncrash one call deeper** and produce another red run, looking like progress while the capability stays\nbroken.\n\n`TicketTransport` declares six members (`create_issue`, `get_issue`, `update_issue`,\n`transition_issue_by_name`, `add_label`, `search_issues`); the core reaches for twenty-one distinct\ntransport members. The port describes under a third of its own requirement. That is exactly why `isinstance` conformance, the backend contract suite,\nand 1636 reconciler unit tests were all green against a transport that cannot complete a writing pass.\n**Conformance to an incomplete port proves less than it appears to** — the same class as J6's\n`add_label` defect, where the only oracle was a fake mirroring the author's assumption.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. TRIAGE each of the twelve to one of IMPLEMENT / NOT-APPLICABLE / UNREACHABLE-BY-CALL-SITE, with\n   evidence (see the triage section above — `get_parent_map` and `set_reporter` both break the naive\n   rule). Then implement the IMPLEMENT set **through pycontribs/jira**, which the DC transport already uses\n   exclusively (no hand-rolled REST anywhere in it; the only `requests` import is for exception\n   TYPES). Every one of the twelve is reachable via the library — but its API is SPLIT across\n   `jira.JIRA` and `jira.resources.Issue`, and scanning only the former is exactly how J6 shipped the\n   `add_label` defect (`add_field_value` lives on Issue, not the client) and how this plan's own first\n   triage wrongly concluded three members had 'no helper'. `Issue.update(fields=…, update=…)` covers\n   parent, reporter and label-removal. Read each Cloud counterpart for its CONTRACT, but do not assume\n   its endpoint or pagination is portable — `set_entity_property` is\n   Cloud's alias for `set_issue_property`, which PUTs to\n   `/rest/api/3/issue/{key}/properties/{prop}` with the value sent **verbatim as the body** — an\n   earlier Cloud bug (0b27) wrapped it in `{\"value\": ...}` and silently stored the wrong shape, so\n   copy the corrected contract, not the intuition. DC is REST **v2**.\n2. Where a method is genuinely not applicable to DC, it must raise a clear `NotImplementedError`\n   naming the DC limitation — never be left absent to fail as `AttributeError` mid-pass. Each such\n   decision needs evidence, not assumption.\n3. Make absence detectable AT CONSTRUCTION rather than at some call site months later. `TicketTransport`\n   is currently `class TicketTransport(Protocol)` with **no `@runtime_checkable`**, so\n   `isinstance(x, TicketTransport)` raises `TypeError` rather than returning `False` — verified\n   directly, and it means the naive \"just isinstance it\" mechanism does not work. So: add\n   `@runtime_checkable` to `TicketTransport` (matching `Backend` and the capability protocols, which\n   already carry it), declare the required members on it, and assert conformance where a backend is\n   BUILT — `_build_jira_datacenter_backend` in `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` and the Cloud\n   equivalent — so a missing member fails loudly at construction.\n4. Add a STRUCTURAL TEST that every transport member the core reaches for is declared on the port.\n   It must collect EVERY attribute access on a `client`/`transport` receiver, not just call forms:\n   a call-form-only audit silently under-counts (see the methodological note below) and would let the\n   thirteenth member slip in unnoticed. This is what stops the class of defect recurring on the next\n   backend.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** implement `set_entity_property` alone. It fixes one crash, leaves eleven,\nand leaves the port still misdescribing what the core needs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — implement (or\n  explicitly `NotImplementedError`) the ELEVEN unguarded absentees listed in the audit above, plus a\n  deliberate decision on `get_parent_map`. **NOT just `set_entity_property`** — implementing one\n  method moves the crash one call deeper and produces another red live run.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py` — add `@runtime_checkable` to `TicketTransport`\n  (it currently lacks it, so `isinstance` against it raises `TypeError`) and declare the members the\n  core actually requires.\n- **`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py`** — `_identity_of` (:163) builds the\n  canonical identity from `raw.get(\"accountId\")` ONLY, so a DC reporter yields `account_id=None` and\n  the reporter mutation re-emits on every pass. It must also carry DC's `name`. This is a CORE module,\n  not an adapter one — the shape is vendor-neutral and both adapters feed it. Required by the\n  reporter-idempotence AC; without it that AC cannot pass.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py` — IN SCOPE after all, for the\n  EXCEPTION model, not for identity. (Identity needs no change: the seam already returns the DC\n  username because 5f48 mints DC identities under provider `jira`. I wrongly marked this file\n  NOT MODIFIED in an earlier revision on that basis — the reviewer caught it.) Line 119 catches only\n  `urllib.error.HTTPError` around `client.set_reporter`, so a DC `JIRAError` escapes the soft-\n  degradation handler. Either the transport translates, or this handler widens — decide once, in the\n  Approach, and apply the SAME decision at both sites.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_differ.py` — `_safe_get_issue` (:138-141) classifies\n  404 → `_DELETED` off `urllib.error.HTTPError.code`. A DC `JIRAError` matches no handler, so\n  deletion detection breaks AND the exception escapes. Must be covered by the same translation\n  decision.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` and\n  `adapters/jira/backend.py` — assert transport conformance in the FACTORY, where the backend is\n  built, so a missing member fails loudly at construction rather than at a call site later.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/backend_support.py` — `FakeTransport` gains whatever members the\n  widened port declares, since it is the contract suite's stand-in and must satisfy it.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — a structural test for the audit: every transport attribute the\n  core reaches for is declared on the Protocol.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py` — already written by J7; it is the oracle and\n  must go from FAIL to PASS unmodified. **Do not weaken it to accommodate the fix.**\n\n## Testing\n\n- unit: each implemented method issues the right REST call, asserted against a fake client. For\n  `set_entity_property` this includes\n  that the property KEY and VALUE reach the endpoint intact, since a silently wrong shape breaks\n  correlation without raising.\n- structural: the core-call-sites-vs-Protocol audit is a TEST, not a one-off grep, so a future core\n  module reaching for an undeclared transport method fails CI. Give it teeth with a synthetic case,\n  mirroring `test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py::test_checker_flags_a_synthetic_violation`.\n- live: J7's `test_reconcile_pass.py` must pass in the `jira-dc-harness` CI job.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] ALL TWELVE absent members carry a RECORDED VERDICT — implemented, `NotImplementedError` naming\n      the DC limitation, or documented as unreachable-by-call-site with the upstream decision named —\n      never simply absent: `delete_issue`, `delete_issue_link`, `get_comments`, `get_issue_by_rest`,\n      `get_issue_links`, `get_parent_map`, `remove_label`, `set_entity_property`,\n      `set_issue_property`, `set_parent`, `set_reporter`, `validate_assignee_exists`\n- [ ] the acceptance bar is NOT \"no `AttributeError`\". For each of the SEVEN members whose call sites\n      swallow `Exception` at every site, and the THREE mixed ones, a test asserts the member is\n      actually PRESENT on the transport — because absence at those sites produces silent degradation,\n      so \"the pass converged\" and \"no exception escaped\" are both true today while nothing works\n- [ ] a test asserts a DC transport with a member REMOVED is rejected at construction (not at a later\n      call site), proving the guard fires\n- [ ] `get_parent_map` is implemented against DC REST **v2** offset pagination\n      (`POST /rest/api/2/search`, `startAt`/`maxResults`) or explicitly declined — NOT copied from\n      Cloud, whose `/rest/api/3/search/jql` cursor endpoint rejects `startAt` with HTTP 400 and does\n      not exist on DC\n- [ ] `set_reporter` is implemented on the DC transport as\n      `issue.update(fields={\"reporter\": {\"name\": …}})`, AND `inbound_fields._identity_of` carries\n      DC's `name` into the canonical identity. `dispatch_apply_phases.py` is NOT modified — the\n      identity seam already returns the DC username\n- [ ] reporter sync is proven IDEMPOTENT on DC: a test asserts that after a successful reporter\n      write, the next differ pass emits NO reporter mutation. This is the criterion that fails today\n      and that `_identity_of` alone fixes — it is not satisfied by \"the write succeeded\"\n- [ ] `set_entity_property` specifically is implemented and unit-tested, with the test asserting the\n      property key and value reach the REST layer INTACT — Cloud bug 0b27 stored `{\"value\": x}`\n      instead of `x`, and a wrong shape breaks correlation WITHOUT raising, so \"it did not error\" is\n      not evidence here\n- [ ] `TicketTransport` is made `@runtime_checkable` and declares the members the core requires —\n      without that decorator `isinstance(x, TicketTransport)` raises `TypeError` rather than\n      returning `False` (verified), so the construction-time guard cannot work at all as a bare\n      isinstance check\n- [ ] the conformance assertion runs where a backend is BUILT (`_build_jira_datacenter_backend` and\n      the Cloud equivalent), so a missing member is a loud construction failure\n- [ ] the DC transport's exceptions are TRANSLATED at the adapter boundary so no core module needs to\n      know `jira.exceptions.JIRAError`. A test asserts a DC 404 reaches `outbound_differ._safe_get_issue`\n      as the SAME classification Cloud produces (`_DELETED`, not `_TRANSPORT_ERROR`, not an escape) —\n      because misclassifying a delete is silent divergence, not a crash\n- [ ] a test asserts a DC `set_reporter` 4xx is caught by the existing soft-degradation handler at\n      `dispatch_apply_phases.py:119` and recorded as `outbound-reporter-not-permitted`, rather than\n      escaping and failing the whole update. The handler's own comment calls 4xx \"the common case\"\n- [ ] `urllib.error.HTTPError` is not the only exception type any CORE module catches from a transport\n      call — asserted structurally, so a third backend cannot reintroduce the same Cloud leak\n- [ ] a structural test asserts every transport member the core reaches for is declared on the port,\n      collecting EVERY attribute access on a `client`/`transport` receiver — not just `client.x(...)`\n      call forms, since a call-form-only audit silently under-counts (it missed `delete_issue_link`\n      and `set_parent` here). Proven to FIRE on a synthetic undeclared member\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a full writing reconcile pass (`bootstrap-strict`) against the J5 harness\n      converges with no unhandled exception. **The covering tests are\n      `test_a_repeated_dc_reconcile_pass_writes_nothing` (test_reconcile_pass.py:226,229) and\n      `test_a_dc_created_ticket_carries_the_shared_jira_provenance` (:286)** — both already run\n      `bootstrap-strict` and both call `_assert_no_unhandled_exception`.\n      NOT `test_dc_reconcile_pass_raises_no_unhandled_exception`, which hard-codes `dry-run` at :207.\n      An earlier draft of this AC named that test and was self-contradictory, since this story also\n      requires the oracle file to stay UNMODIFIED.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] an immediately repeated writing pass writes nothing (idempotence) —\n      J7's `test_a_repeated_dc_reconcile_pass_writes_nothing` passes\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a DC-created ticket carries `creation_channel == 'jira'` — J7's\n      `test_a_dc_created_ticket_carries_the_shared_jira_provenance` passes\n- [ ] the three live tests above pass WITHOUT any edit to `test_reconcile_pass.py` (`git diff` shows\n      that file unchanged) — the oracle is not adjusted to fit the fix\n\n## Note on the moved criteria\n\nThe three `[operator-attested]` live criteria were J7's. They are relocated here, not dropped: J7\ncannot satisfy them (its own scope is landed and verified), and this is the story whose fix makes them\npassable. Epic e369 therefore still requires a proven live writing pass before it closes."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785486182180056001,"uuid":"5ad2e940-d808-46c8-b964-873b02a01f73"}